<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adulis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adulis was an ancient port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. It was the main maritime gateway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the Roman world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> passed through it, and its commerce underpinned Aksum's wealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adulis.jpg" alt="Adulis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Basilica_(8527948107).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Adulis stood on the Gulf of Zula, about 40 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>, and its ruins lie within the modern Eritrean town of Zula. One theory derives its name from the Saho words ado lay, white or clear water, used for settlements with reliable sources of clean water. The town seems to have lain some distance from its harbour, about 20 stades away, and the main anchorage may have been at a site called Gabaza, which archaeologists have proposed placing in the Galala Hills to the south, where two ancient circular watchtowers survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavation has found a late prehistoric settlement beneath the town, dating from the mid-2nd to the early 1st millennium BCE, and Adulis may correspond to Wddt, a region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a> in the geographical lists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Eighteenth Dynasty. Pliny the Elder, the earliest European writer to name it, called it the principal market of the Troglodytae and the people of Aethiopia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, hides and enslaved people among the exports of the interior shipped from it. Roman merchants used the port in the second and third centuries, and in 97 the envoy Gan Ying, according to the Houhan Shu, visited a place called Dou-le, believed to be Adulis.</p><p>In 520 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosmas-indicopleustes">Cosmas Indicopleustes</a> visited the town under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaleb">King Kaleb</a> of Axum and copied two inscriptions there, one on the elephant wars of Ptolemy Euergetes and the Monumentum Adulitanum, in which an Aksumite king boasts of victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Adulis also had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Its end is uncertain: Yuri Kobishchanov argued that Muslims destroyed it, and tradition dates an Arab expedition against it to 640, but the evidence is inconclusive. The port was abandoned sometime around the 8th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>A French mission under Vignaud and Petit surveyed the site in 1840, and in 1868 men of Napier's campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> exposed the foundations of a church. Richard Sundström carried out the first scientific excavations in 1906, uncovering a large building he called the palace of Adulis, and Roberto Paribeni followed in 1907, finding dwellings, pottery and wine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> from the area of modern Aqaba. Francis Anfray's expedition of 1961 and 1962 found a destruction layer. Among the finds is a flask stamped with Saint Menas between two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, perhaps brought by a pilgrim. The National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> has asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to return finds from these excavations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adulis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The guide describes the port of <b>Adulis</b> on the Red Sea coast as a major hub for the export of ivory, tortoiseshell, rhinoceros horn and other African goods, which suggests that Aksum was already overtaking the declining Kingdom of Kush as a leading supplier of African luxury goods…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its wealth came from the Red Sea trade between the Roman world and India, which passed through its port of <b>Adulis</b>, and from a productive farming country in the highlands.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aksum">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Aksum</a></li></ul></section>
